491156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166852 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166852 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166852 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166982 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1bdab585-5331-596a-b7b4-0a1f2915ca75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167717 #edb/ref 1061 33447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d610c8e-cba3-5dd1-81e3-dc49d08dbbe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167725 #edb/ref 1061 33752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d30e0cc8-f5fd-57d0-abd3-963f38723c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167726 #edb/ref 1061 33754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c61c2ab1-44eb-5ab7-a043-c8efa2b4445d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168103 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a differential equation of the form \n\n$$\ny ' = f ( a x + b y ),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants. In this case, we can rewrite the equation as a separable differential equation using the substitution $z= ax+by.$ We then proceed to solve this separable equation.\n\n To see how we apply this method, let's consider the following example: \n\n$$\ny' = (x+y)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = g(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z^2+ 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡z & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the general solution to the equation in terms of $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arctan {(x+y)}  = x + C\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168103 #edb/ref 1065 5345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168103 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f67d9540-b344-56cf-9d27-747a656e8f6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168103 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186170847 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186170847 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186170847 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186170847 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485112 #edb/ref 175