186045417] [17592186180559 #edb/ref 1061 44660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35dfb0c0-efab-5193-92eb-6eb22cc2af9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180560 #edb/ref 1061 44661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30275202-8cd8-51e0-a9e1-92aa287dbe42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180561 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180561 #edb/ref 1061 44664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6802d261-21db-5122-886a-1dda8f3a0db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180562 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180562 #edb/ref 1061 44665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f8aecda-4d54-5528-939f-886f4ab93593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180562 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180563 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180563 #edb/ref 1061 44668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180563 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21d1cffb-5a12-5ee7-ad2a-181e2a8d4474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180563 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180568 #edb/ref 1061 44704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35abe35f-9e29-539b-aaa5-a2546fd2fbfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1061 44705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ec4662b-6870-532f-83f9-f7a9cbe5504e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180570 #edb/ref 1061 44723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d71cf118-7383-5aea-a47c-8359fc7dffe4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180757 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is an affine transformation. The **inverse** of $\\mathbf T$ (if it exists) is an affine transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathbf T^{-1} (\\mathbf T(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf T (\\mathbf T^{-1}(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf u$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf u\\in\\mathbb R^2.$ In other words, the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ reverses the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNot all affine transformations are invertible. So how can we determine whether a given affine transformation is invertible?\n\nTo answer this, recall that an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ can be expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. We can reverse the action of $\\mathbf T$ by making $\\mathbf u$ the subject of this equation, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ {A}^{-1}\\cdot Au & ={A}^{-1}\\cdot (x-b) \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b) \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis equation gives us a method of computing the inverse of an affine transformation. Additionally, we have the following:\n\n*An affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if the matrix $A$ that represents the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is invertible, that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180757 #edb/ref 1065 7697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180757 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf99caa7-1e47-5fea-87ac-9b91151af127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180757 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180758 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186180758 #edb/ref 1065 7698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180758 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c4c88a3e-6514-5b56-ab23-306164b0a3a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180758 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Inverse of an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180761 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the square in the $uv$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8cc0923da86a1a1ac6b666893397263ef3865448080500caa6f17510547d901.png)\n\nWe wish to explore the image of this square under an affine transformation $$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},$$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors.\n\nSimilar to linear transformations, there are three possibilities depending on the value of $\\det(A)$.\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular ($\\det(A)\\neq 0$). In this case, our square is mapped to a parallelogram.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane, we get the following parallelogram:\n\n![](images/36/36da067393fa53f4296373d65e3c6177a54d1a901824188687b4a74ea171afdf.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ has at least one nonzero entry. In this case, our square is mapped to a line segment.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following line segment:\n\n![](images/5e/5e5481e5b2f2218a6097020e260dc991cde05574372c724aa07527458257d94c.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ is the zero matrix. In this case, our square is mapped to a point on the plane.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following point.\n\n![](images/00/0072c5e06674f79f82e771744fda217e6558830f7a6e4ab387aa9dbf4dda09bc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186180761 #edb/ref 1065 7706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180761 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "375d6591-08dd-5dbf-b36a-28013839a28b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180761 #edb/ref 1067 "Image Types Formed by Affine Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180762 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, if we consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the mapping\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, then we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases: \n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane (namely, the point with position vector $\\mathbf b$)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180762 #edb/ref 1065 7710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180762 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "560907bc-354a-5693-a8df-e6824454755d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180762 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary of Image Types" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241454 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2}{(x+y+2)^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241454 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241454 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f3aab70-1877-5ee8-a896-a828fe6ad391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241996 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241996 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241996 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a488a6a-c562-5116-a808-9606ad5fcd65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242059 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{(x+y+2)^{3}}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242059 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242059 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61f69b05-6c80-5dbd-8fb8-f0aead30c675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242122 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{4}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242122 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242122 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "68e92ed8-70bc-5a3e-a2e3-29f9b53dd4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242175 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f9114ac-eb6e-5022-a9dc-0dfb399af7d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242544 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242544 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242544 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9c3ea3a5-0dd3-5cea-8cf5-a5dedd54b5a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242858 #edb/ref 1080 "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bfe35dec-6d42-59f5-8111-b8e850cce8b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242971 #edb/ref 1080 "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242971 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1